1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,672
Three weeks ago, a founder named Dana Whitfield sat down with her media buyer, Sam,

2
00:00:04,672 --> 00:00:07,068
and four ad variants generated from one brief.

3
00:00:07,068 --> 00:00:09,203
Each of them looked at the same four ads.

4
00:00:09,203 --> 00:00:11,025
Each picked a different one to win.

5
00:00:11,025 --> 00:00:12,952
Neither had seen a single result yet.

6
00:00:12,952 --> 00:00:17,170
This lecture is hands-on, and like the last one said, it's the one worth actually

7
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:17,482
doing.

8
00:00:17,482 --> 00:00:21,857
I'm going to show you the same product, and the same four variants Dana and Sam saw.

9
00:00:21,857 --> 00:00:25,866
You're going to pick the one you think wins, before I show you what they each

10
00:00:25,866 --> 00:00:27,689
guessed and what actually happened.

11
00:00:27,689 --> 00:00:30,605
You need nothing but somewhere to write one letter down.

12
00:00:30,605 --> 00:00:32,688
No spreadsheet, no ad account, no tools.

13
00:00:32,688 --> 00:00:37,010
Just a guess you're willing to be wrong about, because being wrong in the next five

14
00:00:37,010 --> 00:00:41,176
minutes is a much cheaper lesson than being wrong with a media budget behind it.

15
00:00:41,726 --> 00:00:42,954
Meet Harlan Supply Co.

16
00:00:42,954 --> 00:00:47,531
You'll see this account again in almost every section from here on, so the numbers

17
00:00:47,531 --> 00:00:49,429
on screen are worth a second look.

18
00:00:49,429 --> 00:00:53,950
Fifty-eight dollars a pack, thirty-four dollars of margin left after landed cost,

19
00:00:53,950 --> 00:00:55,904
fulfilment, and payment processing.

20
00:00:55,904 --> 00:01:00,258
That thirty-four dollars is what has to cover whatever it costs to acquire the

21
00:01:00,258 --> 00:01:01,932
customer, and everything else.

22
00:01:01,932 --> 00:01:06,118
Dana, the founder, spent twelve years as an ER nurse before she built this.

23
00:01:06,118 --> 00:01:09,858
Her reason is on screen: three ruined pairs of socks in one winter.

24
00:01:09,858 --> 00:01:14,491
Her media buyer, Sam, joined part time a few weeks ago, fifteen hours a week, after

25
00:01:14,491 --> 00:01:19,068
running accounts at two other small brands that never had a testing system either.

26
00:01:19,068 --> 00:01:22,473
Between them, they generated four ad variants from one brief.

27
00:01:22,473 --> 00:01:24,762
Hold onto that thirty-four dollar number.

28
00:01:24,762 --> 00:01:25,878
It's not decoration.

29
00:01:25,878 --> 00:01:30,399
Every test budget, every kill rule (the line that says stop spending on a loser),

30
00:01:30,399 --> 00:01:34,865
and every scaling decision for the rest of this course has to fit inside it, and

31
00:01:34,865 --> 00:01:39,442
you'll watch it get spent down, defended, and occasionally nearly blown across the

32
00:01:39,442 --> 00:01:40,614
next eleven sections.

33
00:01:40,614 --> 00:01:44,242
Here are the four variants Dana and Sam generated from one brief.

34
00:01:44,792 --> 00:01:46,027
Read all four properly.

35
00:01:46,027 --> 00:01:46,565
I'll wait.

36
00:01:46,565 --> 00:01:49,788
A leads with Dana's own story: credible, specific, and true.

37
00:01:49,788 --> 00:01:54,246
B opens on the pain the customer already feels, no product mentioned until the last

38
00:01:54,246 --> 00:01:54,515
beat.

39
00:01:54,515 --> 00:01:58,597
C is the spec, stated plainly, no story at all, aimed at someone who already

40
00:01:58,597 --> 00:02:01,444
half-believes the category and just wants the number.

41
00:02:01,444 --> 00:02:05,956
D reads like something a customer filmed on their break, not something a brand made,

42
00:02:05,956 --> 00:02:08,696
and it's the cheapest of the four to have produced.

43
00:02:08,696 --> 00:02:12,993
Each of those is a real, defensible bet about what makes someone stop scrolling.

44
00:02:12,993 --> 00:02:14,873
None of them is a bad bet on paper.

45
00:02:14,873 --> 00:02:18,311
That's what makes this exercise worth doing instead of skipping.

46
00:02:18,311 --> 00:02:19,010
Now pick one.

47
00:02:19,010 --> 00:02:21,534
Which variant do you think won the most orders?

48
00:02:21,534 --> 00:02:23,683
Say a letter out loud, or write it down.

49
00:02:23,683 --> 00:02:27,282
Commit before the next slide, or this exercise teaches you nothing.

50
00:02:27,832 --> 00:02:32,081
Here's what Dana and Sam actually guessed, before either of them saw a result.

51
00:02:32,081 --> 00:02:33,661
Dana picked A, her own story.

52
00:02:33,661 --> 00:02:34,641
Of course she did.

53
00:02:34,641 --> 00:02:38,182
It's true, it's specific, and it's the reason the company exists.

54
00:02:38,182 --> 00:02:40,198
If you'd lived it, you'd back it too.

55
00:02:40,198 --> 00:02:41,450
Sam picked C, the spec.

56
00:02:41,450 --> 00:02:46,026
In his experience running other accounts, a concrete number usually beats a feeling,

57
00:02:46,026 --> 00:02:48,695
and he's not wrong that this pattern often holds.

58
00:02:48,695 --> 00:02:51,528
Notice what already happened before a single ad ran.

59
00:02:51,528 --> 00:02:55,995
Two people who know this brand better than anyone alive, and who each have a real,

60
00:02:55,995 --> 00:02:59,808
defensible reason for their pick, disagree with each other completely.

61
00:02:59,808 --> 00:03:01,224
That's not a training gap.

62
00:03:01,224 --> 00:03:05,746
That's the coin flip from two lectures ago, happening in real time, to people whose

63
00:03:05,746 --> 00:03:06,781
actual job this is.

64
00:03:07,331 --> 00:03:11,234
Here's the twist, and I'm going to hold the actual numbers back for now.

65
00:03:11,234 --> 00:03:14,541
You'll see them in Section 8, with the real test behind them.

66
00:03:14,541 --> 00:03:16,709
The variant that ended up winning was D.

67
00:03:16,709 --> 00:03:17,360
The raw one.

68
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:21,534
The one that doesn't read like an ad at all, and the one neither Dana nor Sam

69
00:03:21,534 --> 00:03:23,920
picked, ranked, or even seriously discussed.

70
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:27,172
It was also, worth noting, the cheapest of the four to make.

71
00:03:27,172 --> 00:03:31,455
With hindsight, you could tell a story about why: it looks like a phone someone

72
00:03:31,455 --> 00:03:36,009
actually holds, not a studio anyone dressed, so it survives the first half second of

73
00:03:36,009 --> 00:03:38,611
a scroll differently than a polished claim does.

74
00:03:38,611 --> 00:03:41,268
But notice the word "hindsight" in that sentence.

75
00:03:41,268 --> 00:03:45,605
It's a story you build after, never a prediction you could have made before, and

76
00:03:45,605 --> 00:03:47,068
that's precisely the point.

77
00:03:47,068 --> 00:03:50,538
Would that story have convinced Dana or Sam before the test ran?

78
00:03:50,538 --> 00:03:53,953
Almost certainly not, and that's not a knock on either of them.

79
00:03:54,503 --> 00:03:58,733
I want to be precise about what just happened, because it's easy to draw the wrong

80
00:03:58,733 --> 00:03:59,094
lesson.

81
00:03:59,094 --> 00:04:02,137
Dana and Sam didn't fail because they're bad at their jobs.

82
00:04:02,137 --> 00:04:06,315
They failed because "which of these looks like a winner" is not a question a look

83
00:04:06,315 --> 00:04:09,409
can answer, no matter how experienced the person looking is.

84
00:04:09,409 --> 00:04:13,381
It's the same category error as judging a lottery ticket by how confident the

85
00:04:13,381 --> 00:04:14,361
numbers on it look.

86
00:04:14,361 --> 00:04:17,043
Notice, too, that you probably weren't right either.

87
00:04:17,043 --> 00:04:21,272
If you were, treat it as a genuinely lucky guess, not a skill to trust on the next

88
00:04:21,272 --> 00:04:25,140
four variants, because the same test run again would draw a different card.

89
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:27,565
This course will not teach you to guess better.

90
00:04:27,565 --> 00:04:31,278
Nobody can teach that, honestly, because there's nothing there to learn.

91
00:04:31,278 --> 00:04:35,404
It teaches you to build the brief, the batch, the pre-flight check, and the test

92
00:04:35,404 --> 00:04:38,551
that finds the answer without ever needing your guess at all.

93
00:04:39,101 --> 00:04:39,996
That's Section 0.

94
00:04:39,996 --> 00:04:44,368
You've got the idea, you've felt it fail on real people who are good at their jobs,

95
00:04:44,368 --> 00:04:48,530
and you've met the account this whole course is going to follow: one brief, one

96
00:04:48,530 --> 00:04:52,744
batch, a killed draft, a real test, a tired winner, and eventually a scaled one.

97
00:04:52,744 --> 00:04:55,062
Here's the one thing to do before Section 1.

98
00:04:55,062 --> 00:04:59,381
Write down, for your own product, the angle you'd never pick: the raw, offbeat, or

99
00:04:59,381 --> 00:05:03,069
slightly embarrassing version you'd normally cut before anyone saw it.

100
00:05:03,069 --> 00:05:06,124
Variant D looked exactly like that, right up until it won.

101
00:05:06,124 --> 00:05:06,914
Keep that line.

102
00:05:06,914 --> 00:05:11,181
It's the first entry Section 2's hook matrix, artifact B02, is built to make sure

103
00:05:11,181 --> 00:05:13,604
you actually test instead of quietly deleting.

104
00:05:13,604 --> 00:05:17,713
Section 1 is next, and it answers the question you're probably already asking.

105
00:05:17,713 --> 00:05:22,032
If taste doesn't work, what does, and how many variants does finding a real winner

106
00:05:22,032 --> 00:05:22,770
actually take?

107
00:05:22,770 --> 00:05:26,668
The answer is a number, not a feeling, and it's smaller than you'd expect.
